// Ranges whose parts do not agree once the loop has been rewritten.
//
// 'each' has no type rules of its own: the parser lowers it into a comparison,
// an increment and possibly a '+=', and it is those that are type checked. So
// the diagnostics below name operators nothing in this file spells -- '+++' is
// the post-increment appended to the loop body, and '+' is the '+=' a 'by'
// step becomes.
//
// They are reported on the range that produced them. They used to land on the
// enclosing function's closing '}': parseEach builds those nodes after
// parseExprBlock has consumed the whole loop body, so they carried whatever
// position the lexer had reached, which is the token after the body. Each
// synthesized node is now positioned on the range expression instead.

// The two bounds are the operands of one comparison, so they must be of one
// type. An i32 lower bound and a u32 upper bound have no '<=' between them, and
// the condition is then untyped for the 'while' that wants a boolean.
fn mismatchedBounds() {
  each i in 0 <= 5u {    //~ ErrorNoCandidate:15 "No method declared by `<=`"
  }                      //~^ ErrorInvType:15 follow-on
}

// Whatever the bounds are, the loop variable is stepped by the increment
// operator, so a type with no '++' cannot be iterated over however well its
// comparison type checks.
fn notIncrementable() {
  each b in false < true {  //~ ErrorNoMbr:19 "`+++` not found"
  }
}

// A 'by' step becomes a '+=' on the loop variable, so it has to be a type the
// variable can be added to. An unsigned step does not add to a signed one.
fn mismatchedStep() {
  each i in 0 < 5 by 1u {  //~ ErrorNoCandidate:15 "No method declared by `+`"
  }
}
